Mill Extras

Optional files for Mill that need nothing from it, and that it needs nothing from: Mill runs
the same without them.

Web Fonts
  Mill's own fonts, Mill Serif and Mill Mono: a font suitcase (type FFIL, creator MiLL) of their
  bitmaps, their outlines, and the code that draws the outlines. Put it in the "Mill Plug-Ins"
  folder beside Mill. At startup Mill opens each FFIL file there, read-only, and keeps it open;
  the font settings (General, Proportional font and Fixed font) then list the families in every
  such file with the System's, and Mill Serif and Mill Mono are the defaults (without the
  suitcase, Times and Courier). It is skipped when Shift is held at startup, as Plug-Ins are.
  What is in it is checked first (every table of each FONT and FOND inside the resource, every
  FONT the FOND names); a family that fails, or a file that can't be opened, is left out, and
  Mill uses the System's fonts as if it weren't there.

  Mill Serif and Mill Mono are Web Fonts' alone, on every System, with TrueType or without: the
  bitmaps at 9, 10, 12, and 14 point, the outlines from 18 to 72 point, and the nearest of those
  for any other size (16 point is drawn at 14). Times, Courier, or any font chosen by name or
  named by a page's FACE is the System's alone. Lines in Mill Serif and Mill Mono are as tall as
  their own bitmaps or outlines make them, the same on every System.

  Bitmaps: each family in regular, bold, and italic (bold italic is made by QuickDraw):
    Mill Serif   Adobe's X11 bitmaps of the Times design: timR, timB, timI
    Mill Mono    Adobe's X11 bitmaps of the Courier design: courR, courB, courO (the oblique
                 is the italic)
  Each size slot takes its strike from the "files" entry in fonts.json (a source and a BDF file
  for each slot). Now: slot 9 is the 75 dpi 10 pixel strike, 10 is the 100 dpi 8 point (11
  pixels), and 12 and 14 are the 75 dpi 12 and 14 pixel strikes. The strikes are Adobe's
  hand-tuned bitmaps, converted, not redrawn: each Mac Roman character 32 to 255 is found by its
  Unicode value in the BDF file (its encoding is ISO 10646-1) and put in its Mac Roman place. The
  characters a strike lacks show as the missing glyph: the fi and fl ligatures and the Apple
  logo in every strike, and, in most strikes, ten math symbols (infinity, partial, sum, product,
  pi, integral, Omega, root, almost equal, increment) and the lozenge; build.py prints the list.

  Outlines ('Msfn' resources, TrueType data the Font Manager doesn't see, listed in 'Mfnt' 128),
  each family in regular, bold, and italic; bold italic is the italic made bold:
    Mill Serif   Tinos 1.340 (fonts/tinos)
    Mill Mono    Bitstream's Courier 10 Pitch (fonts/bitstream), converted from Type 1; it
                 lacks 13 math symbols and the lozenge
  outlines.py reduces each for Mill: Mac Roman only, no hints or layout tables, curves
  simplified within 1/8 pixel at 18 point, 256 units per em; about 15K a face. build.py checks
  each result's SHA-256, so a build gives the same fonts every time (fontTools 4.68.0).

  Code: the Plug-In in ../../plugins/fonts (MPlg 128 and its MIfc), which draws the outlines a
  letter at a time as pages need them and keeps up to 24K of drawn letters, purgeable.

  It can also be opened with Font/DA Mover; the Bold and Italic strikes are FONTs of their own
  (see Styles). Font/DA Mover leaves the outlines and the code behind.

Making it
  python3 build.py [--config fonts.json] [--out dist] [--cache cache] [--preview] [--styles]
                   [--code "../../plugins/fonts/build/Web Fonts.flt"] [--table]
  Needs Python 3 with freetype-py 2.5.1, macresources 1.2, and fontTools 4.68.0 (and Pillow for
  --preview). ../../build.sh disk runs it with --styles and --code, after building the code, in
  the python:3.12-slim image pinned by digest, with those versions.
  It reads the fonts from the archives in fonts/ (--download: from the addresses in fonts.json,
  the X11 releases from x.org and Tinos's three files from google/fonts), checks each file's
  SHA-256, converts the BDF strikes and the outlines, and writes:
    Web Fonts.bin           MacBinary, with the fonts, 'vers' 1 and 2, and 'TEXT' 128 (about)
                             and 'TXGZ' 129 (the license, gzipped)
    Web Fonts Read Me.txt   the same, with the license, for the folder it goes in
  The same sources make the same bytes: the files are dated 1997-12-25 (SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH, if
  set, instead) and the license's gzip header is fixed.
  A family with "ttf" instead of
  "files" in fonts.json is rendered with FreeType (a "hinting" entry then picks, per size, the
  font's own hints or the autohinter); the default configuration has none.

Styles
  Both families have real Bold and Italic strikes at 9, 10, 12, and 14 point. They are FONTs of
  their own, found through the FOND's association table at an ID in an unused family; the table
  lists the slot sizes with the strikes inside them. Underline is QuickDraw's.
  build.py refuses a style whose real strikes are not the family's smallest sizes with no gap: on a
  Mac Plus, real Bold at only 18, 24, and 36 point hung the machine, and the cause is not known.
  --table prints what each part costs:

  FONT resource bytes          9      10      12      14   total
  Mill Serif plain          2568    2594    3240    3854   12256
  Mill Serif bold           2620    2646    3300    4098   12664
  Mill Serif italic         2698    2698    3300    4170   12866
  Mill Mono plain           2228    2228    2862    3618   10936
  Mill Mono bold            2426    2426    3114    3874   11840
  Mill Mono italic          2404    2360    3142    3778   11684
  Mill Serif pixels           10      11      12      14
  Mill Mono pixels            10      11      12      14
  Mill Serif plain          15148  Msfn
  Mill Serif bold           15064  Msfn
  Mill Serif italic         15736  Msfn
  Mill Mono plain           16000  Msfn
  Mill Mono bold            15772  Msfn
  Mill Mono italic          17488  Msfn
